data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_04f7f2b6a7b01fec006adb66cf43a087d1aa18a76993604123","logprobs":[],"output_index":0,"sequence_number":238,"text":"During sleep, memories reactivated in the hippocampus are thought to be transferred to the neocortex for longer-term storage [2]. [2] reports that slow oscillations, spindles, and hippocampal ripples interact in a finely timed sequence that may support this redistribution [2].\n\nMuehlroth et al. report that fast spindles precisely coupled to slow oscillations promote memory consolidation, while less precise, “aged” coupling coincides with worse consolidation [1]. Diekelmann explains that sleep supports memory stability and reduces forgetting, rather than enabling new declarative or procedural learning during sleep [3].\n\nCaveat: The passages describe proposed mechanisms and evidence for sleep-related memory consolidation, but do not establish every step of the process.\n\nSources:\n[1] p3: \"a precise ‘youth-like’ SO-fast SP coupling is beneficial for memory consolidation\"\n[2] p2: \"finetuned temporal interplay between slow oscillations, spindles and ripples presumably mediates the redistribution\"\n[3] p3: \"sleep as well as memory manipulations during sleep enhance memory in the sense of less forgetting\""}

